<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lae is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> and capital of Morobe Province, at the mouth of the Markham River on the Huon Gulf. It grew in the 1920s and 1930s as the air base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulolo">Bulolo</a> goldfields, and Amelia Earhart took off from there on her last flight in 1937. Destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, it was rebuilt as the country's main industrial centre and port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lae.jpg" alt="Lae" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lae_City_and_Markham_River_(aerial_view).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lae lies near the delta of the Markham River on the northern coast of Huon Gulf and is the capital of Morobe Province. It stands at the start of the Highlands Highway, the main land transport corridor between the Highlands Region and the coast. In German it was called Preußen-Reede and later Lehe. The city lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire in the Ramu-Markham Fault Zone, where tectonic plates converge at up to 50 millimetres a year, and the Ramu-Markham zone has produced large thrust earthquakes, among them one of magnitude 6.4 in April 1999. Its tropical rainforest climate brings about 4,500 millimetres of rain a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Lucas divides the history of Lae into four periods: the mission phase from 1886 to 1920, the gold phase from 1926 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and agricultural phase until 1965, and the industrial boom from 1965, with the opening of the Highlands Highway. On 12 July 1886 the German missionary Johann Flierl sailed to Simbang and arrived at Lae shortly afterwards. After the First World War Eastern New Guinea came under Australian control, and in 1921 the gold prospector Cecil John Levien became District Officer of Morobe.</p><p>In 1927 he arranged for an airstrip at Lae to serve the gold mines around Wau. Lae was declared a town on 31 March 1931 and became the prototype for New Guinean towns built around airstrips, with cargo flown from Lae to the Wau goldfields.</p><p>In July 1937 the American aviator Amelia Earhart was last seen flying out of Lae on her way back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. After volcanic eruptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> that year it was decided to move the capital of the Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> to Lae, but the war prevented it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the town on 8 March 1942, and Lae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> and Salamaua became its major bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. The Battle of the Bismarck Sea in March 1943 was fought over a Japanese attempt to reinforce Lae by sea. After weeks of fierce fighting in the Salamaua–Lae campaign the town fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> on 16 September. It was proclaimed a city in 1972, and its postwar growth was tied to the highlands, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> needed a port and then a road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lae is the industrial hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> and its largest cargo port. The Papua New Guinea University of Technology stands 8 kilometres outside the city. Lae War Cemetery, established in 1944, holds the remains of over 2,800 soldiers, many of whom died in the Salamaua–Lae campaign, and is the resting place of two Victoria Cross recipients. The city is nicknamed the Garden City.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
